 "pattern": "Hash map lookup",
 "statement": "Given a list of integers `nums` and a number `target`, find the two numbers that add up to `target` and return their **indices**. Exactly one pair works, and you may not use the same element twice.",
 "example": {
  "input": "nums = [3,11,5,6], target = 9",
  "output": "[0,3]",
  "why": "nums[0] + nums[3] = 3 + 6 = 9."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "nums = [2,7,11,15], target = 9",
   "output": "[0,1]"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [3,2,4], target = 6",
   "output": "[1,2]"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [3,3], target = 6",
   "output": "[0,1]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Checking every pair works but costs O(n²): for each number we search the whole list for its partner.",
  "For a number `n` the partner is fixed: it must be `target − n`. So the only question is *have I already seen that value?*",
  "A hash map answers that instantly. Walk the list once: look up the partner, and if it is not there yet, store the current number with its index."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def twoSum(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> List[int]:\n        prevMap = {}  # val -> index\n\n        for i, n in enumerate(nums):\n            diff = target - n\n            if diff in prevMap:\n                return [prevMap[diff], i]\n            prevMap[n] = i",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "One pass over the list; every map lookup and insert is O(1) on average.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "In the worst case the map stores almost every number before the pair appears."
 },
